declarations.description:
Create one Pro Flash pixel-art image with an optional left-box style image.

Custom sizes (Beta) accept width and height from 16 to 256 in multiples of 4 pixels.
Query get_pro_flash_capabilities for native presets.
The style image accepts {url}, {base64}, or {source_image_id}; prefer URLs
for uploaded images. Style art must fit the native canvas without resizing.
For several assets of one game, pass the same anchor sprite as style_image on
every call (e.g. the hero's south rotation URL from get_character); without it
each call picks its own look.
style_options selects color_palette, outline, detail and shading independently.
Default style uses existing PixelLab art. For a console or palette look (1-bit,
Game Boy, PICO-8, any palette from Lospec) pass `palette` as its hex colours plus
`palette_name`: the image is drawn for the palette and every pixel of the result
is one of its colours; the style image keeps lending its look. Returns one image.
Failed pixel grids use image-to-pixel-art with fixer on the generated box.
Poll get_image(job_id); use source_image_id after completion.

Cost: by LONGEST SIDE — 5 generations up to 96px, 6 from 100 to 208, 9 from
212 to 256, for one image. A non-square canvas goes by its longer side, so
128x72 is 6 and 32x96 is 5. This is the whole Pro Flash ladder (create, edit
and inpaint charge the same), and it is NOT the Pro tools' 10/15/25 tier.
No parameter caps a charge, and the quote is labelled provisional rather
than a contractual cap — but the ladder above is what actually gets billed,
so treat those figures as the price.
get_pro_flash_capabilities(operation=, width=, height=) prices an exact size
for free before you spend.

PixelLab AI-powered pixel art generation service.

Use of this service is subject to PixelLab's Terms of Service:
https://pixellab.ai/termsofservice

This MCP server provides programmatic access to generate:
- Characters with multiple directional views
- Character animations
- Isometric tiles
- Top-down tilesets for game maps
- Sidescroller tilesets for platformer games
- Music and sound effects for games

All generation operations are non-blocking and return job IDs for status tracking.
Requires PixelLab subscription (with credit fallback support).
A tool's full reference (every option, prices by size): search_help(tool="<tool name>"), free.

**Pixel tools (free, exact, you are the artist)**: one command line, pixelart_workbench(argv=[...]), inspects, edits, draws and animates pixel art by your own decisions; combine it with the AI tools above. Start with pixelart_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start'], mode='low'|'high') — low acts directly with few tokens, high plans out loud and reviews every frame. Animated effects (spells, hits, explosions, auras): vfx_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start']) — same ids, exports to the user's My effects.

**Git access**: Clone and push game project source code with standard git.
Use `list_projects` to see your projects and their git clone URLs.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_image_pro_flash(args: {
  description: string;
  height?: number;
  // At most this many colours in the image (2-64). With a palette, they are picked from it.
  max_colors?: number | null;
  no_background?: boolean;
  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856", "#88c070", "#e0f8d0"] for Game Boy or ["#000000", "#ffffff"] for 1-bit. The image is drawn for these colours and every pixel of the result is one of them.
  palette?: Array<string> | null;
  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8'); naming it helps the look.
  palette_name?: string | null;
  project_id?: string | null;
  seed?: number;
  // Style anchor. Exactly ONE of its three sources: `url` also accepts an `upload:<id>` from get_upload_url or a data: URL, and `source_image_id` is an image you own (what a Pro Flash job returns). It must fit INSIDE the output canvas both ways — a larger one is refused, so crop it; resizing pixel art is never the answer. Reuse one anchor across calls to keep a set consistent.
  style_image?: {
  base64?: string | null;
  source_image_id?: string | null;
  url?: string | null;
  // How this reference should guide appearance.
  usage_description?: string | null;
} | null;
  // Choose which visual traits to copy from the style image.
  style_options?: { color_palette?: boolean; detail?: boolean; outline?: boolean; shading?: boolean; } | null;
  // Native sizes: 16x16 (experimental), 24x24, 32x32, 32x48, 48x48, 64x64, 96x64, 96x96, 128x128. Custom size (Beta): integer width 16–256 and height 16–256. Both dimensions must be multiples of 4 pixels. Pixel-grid recovery uses image-to-pixel-art with fixer when needed. No native image resizing.
  width?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```
